<reading-questions xml:id="reflections-special-forms">
<title>Reflect on your understanding</title>

<introduction><p>
	In addition to the reflection activities below,
	re-read <xref ref="section-special-forms-terminology" text="type-global-title" />.
	Be sure you understand each of the new definitions introduced in this chapter,
	and spend some time committing them to memory.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>
	<title>Special form patterns</title>
	<p>
	Summarize the patterns in the entries of each of the special forms <term>scalar</term>, <term>diagonal</term>, <term>upper triangular</term>, <term>lower triangular</term>, and <term>symmetric</term>.
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Special forms as coefficient matrices</title>

	<introduction><p>
		Consider each question below for each of the forms <term>scalar</term>, <term>diagonal</term>, <term>upper triangular</term>, and <term>lower triangular</term>.
	</p></introduction>

	<task><p>
		What are the possibilities for the rank of a matrix in that form?
		What properties/patterns does the answer depend on?
	</p></task>

	<task><p>
		How many solutions can a system have where the coefficient matrix has that form?
		What properties/patterns does the answer depend on?
	</p></task>

</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>RREF matrices</title>
	<task><p><alert>True or false:</alert> Every square RREF matrix is upper triangular. </p></task>
	<task><p><alert>True or false:</alert> Every upper triangular matrix is in RREF.     </p></task>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Hierarchy of special forms</title>
	<introduction><p>
		Be sure you can articulate the reasoning behind your answer for each based on the definitions of the forms
		in the <xref ref="section-special-forms-terminology" text="title" /> section for this chapter.
	</p></introduction>
	<task><p><alert>True or false:</alert> Every diagonal matrix is also upper triangular. </p></task>
	<task><p><alert>True or false:</alert> Every diagonal matrix is also lower triangular. </p></task>
	<task><p><alert>True or false:</alert>
		A matrix that is both upper and lower triangular must be diagonal.
	</p></task>
	<task><p><alert>True or false:</alert> Every diagonal matrix is also symmetric.        </p></task>
	<task><p><alert>True or false:</alert> Every scalar matrix is also diagonal.           </p></task>
	<task><p><alert>True or false:</alert> Every scalar matrix is also upper triangular.   </p></task>
	<task><p><alert>True or false:</alert> Every scalar matrix is also lower triangular.   </p></task>
	<task><p><alert>True or false:</alert> Every scalar matrix is also symmetric.          </p></task>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Invertibility of special forms</title>
	<p>
	Remind yourself of the simple condition based on the entries of the matrix by which you can tell whether a scalar matrix is invertible.
	Repeat for diagonal and for triangular matrices.
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="reflection-special-forms-mult-by-diag">
	<title>Multiplying by diagonal</title>
	<p>
	State the pattern in the result of each of the following computation recipes.
	<ol>
		<li><em>diagonal-matrix times matrix</em></li>
		<li><em>matrix times diagonal-matrix</em></li>
	</ol>
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Decomposing diagonal matrices</title>
	<statement><p>
		<xref ref="theorem-elem-matrices-equiv-to-invertible-prod-elem">Statement</xref> of <xref ref="theorem-elem-matrices-equiv-to-invertible" />
		says that every invertible matrix can be expressed as a product of elementary matrices.
		Describe a simple general pattern for how every invertible diagonal matrix can be expressed as a product of elementary matrices.
	</p></statement>
	<hint><p> See <xref ref="reflection-special-forms-mult-by-diag" text="type-local" />. </p></hint>
</exercise>

</reading-questions>
